        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
A multidisciplinary team, known as the Medical Operational Support Team (MOST) adapted a Human Patient Simulator (HPS) for use in physiologic scripting and modeling states specific to the aerospace environment. This model was used to help develop, evaluate, and validate medical procedures for use in space.  The MOST project has supported educational outreach activities since its inception. The MOST team developed an integrated, multimedia medical curriculum. Following validation, it was initiated as a pilot program for students at NASA Johnson Space Center.  The need for strategies to address medical emergencies in space environments was reemphasized in a recent NASA report on Guidelines and Capabilities for Designing Human Missions. 
<P>
This research in astronaut and flight surgeon training has obvious applications to terrestrial medical training. The same philosophy of operationally relevant simulation-based training can be applied to the education programs of both traditional and non-traditional health care providers. In particular, the research into sustainment training will establish the standard for this type of testing. In addition, techniques developed by this team can be adapted by terrestrial health care providers; for example, adaptation of the free-floating intubation technique has been evaluated by paramedics as an alternative method for difficult airways. Modeling results are currently being used to create additional simulator-based patients for use in training anesthesiology students.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study is not available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The work completed to date in the MOST project reflects significant progress toward development of an integrated, simulation-based training program to prepare flight and ground-based personnel to cope effectively with several anticipated health-related emergencies. Investigators reviewed physiological research data, scripting and modeling literature, and scripted HPS physiological models for astronaut subpopulations. They developed, evaluated and validated, in a combination of ground-based analog test environments and during parabolic flight, several procedures relevant to space medicine using the enhanced, space adapted HPS. They further demonstrated the utility of flight-ready HPS and gathered preliminary intubating laryngeal mask airway (ILMA) insertion data in both restrained and free-floating patients They also tested methods for unstowing the on-board defibrillator, evaluated non-clinicians' ability to conduct an in-flight papillary assessment, and evaluated the setup of intravenous fluid infusion system. They implemented and assessed the efficacy of the space-adapted HPS system as a training aid for knowledge and skill acquisition of procedures. Investigators validated skill set training for surgical and non-surgical procedures (e.g., airway management as well as pneumothorax) using the HPS model, developed training regimens for specific medical procedures (e.g., administering anesthesia to patients in the field) in response to requests from NASA personnel, and provided refresher training in emergency medical procedures to NASA personnel.
<p>
        </div>

